          "python",
          "def demonstrate_incompleteness_intuition():\n    \"\"\"\n    The Liar Paradox gives intuition for incompleteness.\n    Consider: \"This statement is not provable\"\n\n    If the system is consistent:\n    - If the statement IS provable, then (by soundness) it's true,\n      meaning it's NOT provable - contradiction!\n    - So it's NOT provable. But then the statement IS true!\n    - We have a true statement that is not provable.\n\n    This is the essence of Godel's construction using\n    Godel numbering to create self-referential statements.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(\"Godel's insight:\")\n    print(\"1. Encode proof system within itself (Godel numbering)\")\n    print(\"2. Construct: G = 'G is not provable in system S'\")\n    print(\"3. If G is provable -> G is true -> G is not provable (contradiction)\")\n    print(\"4. If G is not provable -> G is true (true but unprovable!)\")\n    print(\"5. G is true but unprovable - the system is incomplete\")\n    print()\n    print(\"This applies to any consistent system containing arithmetic\")\n    print(\"It does NOT apply to propositional logic (which IS complete)\")\n\ndemonstrate_incompleteness_intuition()"
        ]
